.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt FORK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fork ,
.Nm vfork ,
.Nm clone
.Nd create a child process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft pid_t
.Fn fork "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn vfork "void"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_clone" "unsigned long flags" "void *stack" "pid_t *parent_tid" "void *tls" "pid_t *child_tid"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn fork
creates a child process with its own process record, address space, and
file descriptor table.
User pages are copied on write: parent and child initially share their
contents, but a write by either process does not change the other's memory.
.Pp
The child inherits the parent's program name, credentials, supplementary
groups, working directory, file creation mask, resource limits, session,
process group, nice value, dumpable flag and no_new_privs bit, and not its
parent-death signal; see
.Xr prctl 2 .
Its CPU times start at zero.
It inherits signal dispositions and the blocked signal mask;
its pending signal set is empty.
The inherited program name remains visible in
.Pa /proc/<pid>/stat
until
.Xr execve 2
replaces it.
.Pp
Inherited descriptors refer to the same open-file descriptions as the
parent's descriptors.
They share file offsets and status flags.
Parent and child can close their descriptors independently.
.Pp
The soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
limit bounds the number of processes with the parent's real user ID,
including the parent.
A parent with real user ID 0 is exempt.
.Pp
.Fn vfork
uses the same kernel handler as
.Fn fork .
Unlike Linux
.Fn vfork ,
it does not suspend the parent or share one address space with the child.
The child has the same copy-on-write address space as a
.Fn fork
child.
.Pp
.Fn clone ,
number 120, creates a child the way
.Fn fork
does, in the form GNU libc's
.Fn fork
uses.
Its i386 arguments are, in order, the flags, a stack pointer for the
child, the address of the parent's copy of the child's ID, a TLS
descriptor, and the address of the child's copy.
The low byte of the flags, the signal the parent receives when the child
exits, must be
.Dv SIGCHLD .
The flags may also contain
.Dv CLONE_PARENT_SETTID ,
which stores the child's ID at
.Fa parent_tid
in the parent's memory;
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_SETTID ,
which stores it at
.Fa child_tid
in the child's memory before the child runs;
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID ,
which records
.Fa child_tid
as the address the child's thread end clears, as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
describes for
.Nm set_tid_address ;
and
.Dv CLONE_DETACHED ,
which is ignored.
A failed store is ignored, as on Linux.
A nonzero
.Fa stack
is the stack pointer the child returns to user mode with.
Every other flag, including those that share memory, files or signal
handlers, and every other exit signal is refused.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 2 for
.Fn fork ,
190 for
.Fn vfork
and 120 for
.Fn clone .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The parent receives the child's process ID; the child receives 0.
On failure, the function returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/forktest.c
creates a child that exits with status 42, waits for it, and checks for a
normal exit with that status.
.Pa tests/ofdprobe.c
checks that a child advances the file offset seen by its parent.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOMEM]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn clone
was given a flag other than those above or an exit signal other than
.Dv SIGCHLD .
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The number of processes with the parent's real user ID has reached the
parent's soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
limit.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate the child's process record, page tables,
user page state, kernel stack, memory region records, or descriptor table.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr exit 2 ,
.Xr getpid 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr waitpid 2
